From da8906fddf72882aab5d2d277fc38cf9c901df7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:53:22 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] linprocfs: real /proc/<pid>/fd directories and a per-jail
 /proc/1

/proc/<pid>/fd was a symlink to /dev/fd for the calling process and to
"unknown" for any other, so Linux programs could not list or open another
process's descriptors.  It is now a directory of the process's
descriptors, as on Linux:

 - readdir lists the open descriptors;
 - readlink(2) of an entry gives the file's path, or pipe:[ino],
   socket:[ino], anon_inode:[eventpoll] (the Linuxulator's epoll is a
   kqueue);
 - open(2) of an entry gives the caller the process's open file.  Writing
   to /proc/<pid>/fd/1 writes to that process's stdout;
 - the caller must be allowed to debug the process (p_candebug), as Linux
   requires; otherwise EACCES.

This is a new pseudofs node type, pfstype_fddir.  Its entries are VCHR
vnodes with VV_READLINK, like fdescfs entries, so that open(2) reaches
VOP_OPEN.  There the file is installed in a spare descriptor and handed to
openatfp() through td_dupfd and ENXIO, and dupfdopen() moves it to the
descriptor open(2) returns.  This is only done for open(2) itself
(td_dupfd is -1) and not in capability mode or with O_RESOLVE_BENEATH,
where openatfp() would not take the descriptor.

In a jail, /proc/1 is now the jail's oldest process.  For a container that
is its first process, which is PID 1 in the container's PID namespace on
Linux; programs write to /proc/1/fd/1 to reach the container's log.  A new
optional pseudofs hook, pi_pidmap, rewrites the pid of a process
directory lookup; such names are not entered in the name cache because
their meaning depends on the caller.  Outside jails /proc/1 is init.

Found with Envoy Gateway: its preStop hook logs to /proc/1/fd/1 and
panicked with ENOENT, so Envoy never drained and every Envoy pod deletion
waited out its 360 s grace period.  With this change the drain runs and
deletion takes 12 s.
